{"name":"Blackened Pearl","hex":"#504d53","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blackened","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Darkened to the point of appearing black; as:\n\n Synonym of charred.","Synonym of charred."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of blacken"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blackened"},{"term":"pearl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shelly concretion, usually rounded, and having a brilliant luster, with varying tints, found in the mantle, or between the mantle and shell, of certain bivalve mollusks, especially in the pearl oysters and river mussels, and sometimes in certain univalves. It is usually due to a secretion of shelly substance around some irritating foreign particle. Its substance is the same as nacre, or mother-of-pearl. Round lustrous pearls are used in jewellery.\nNacre; mother-of-pearl.\n A white color inspired by the shade of pearls.","Nacre; mother-of-pearl."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set or adorn with pearls, or with mother-of-pearl.","To cause to resemble pearls in shape; to make into small round grains."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl","wikidata":"Q43436"},{"title":"Pearl (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color pearl is a pale tint of off-white. It is a representation of the average color of a pearl."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/PerlmuttAusst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_(color)","wikidata":"Q7158021"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/500px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/1280px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg/500px-Georgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Georgian seed pearl gold ring","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeorgian_seed_pearl_gold_ring.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/500px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/1280px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg/500px-Black_pearl_and_his_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A black pearl and a shell of the black-lipped pearl oyster. The iridescent colors originate from nacre layers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3